From a2c90a7d02247fe78aec7353dbf03d6a3c645f8b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sat, 29 Aug 2026 06:27:24 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 1/2] Four features: auto-reconnect, album merge, band
 overview, circle analysis
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Auto-reconnect (js/session.js)
  The queue, the track and the playhead are written back as they change and
  restored on the next launch. Autoplay stays the browser's decision: the
  track is cued and, if `play()` is refused, the readout says so and offers
  one button. A library held by a file input cannot re-acquire its files by
  script, so that case now says "Folder not connected" and arms itself — the
  resume finishes on its own the moment the folder is handed back.

  The playhead is mirrored into localStorage once a second. IndexedDB is the
  right home for the session, but a write started during `pagehide` does not
  land, and a synchronous mirror is the difference between coming back at
  0:02 and coming back at 0:00.

Album merge (js/library.js, js/tags.js)
  Albums split across folders fold back into one entity. The hard case is
  that an untagged rip is never nameless: the tag reader falls back to the
  folder tree, so half of Graduation sitting in Unsorted/Rips arrives
  claiming to be by "Unsorted". Tags now record which fields they had to
  guess, tracks carry the verdict as `namedArtist`, and a guessed artist
  counts as no artist when albums are compared. Two records that both named
  themselves still never merge.

  Merging runs before the artist index is built, so absorbing an album can
  hand its tracks a real artist without leaving a page behind for a folder
  name.

Band overview (js/band.js, js/views.js)
  Off until switched on, and switching it on shows exactly what leaves the
  device. MusicBrainz and Wikipedia, keyless and CORS-enabled, one request a
  second, every answer cached in IndexedDB for a month. Four cards, per-album
  deepening on request, and owned releases linked back into the library.

Circle Analysis Center (js/circles.js, js/stats.js)
  Listening time measured while audio genuinely advances, and drawn as
  area-proportional circles by artist, genre or year. Hover for exact values,
  click to pin and compare, drag to arrange.

File selection
  Individual files can be picked, not just folders, and both routes are
  offered on every browser rather than falling back to whichever API exists.

Tests
  smoke, interactions and perf all pass. The queue-row click test now targets
  a row that is actually on screen: the last row in a virtualised list is
  below the fold, and scrolling to it recycles the node about to be clicked.

