docs/SPEC.md is the deliverable: feature specifications with measurable
acceptance criteria, user flows, the design language, the data model,
feature flags, performance budgets, privacy, accessibility, the API
surface, migration notes and a changelog-ready summary. Every criterion
that says "verified" is one a suite actually asserts.

Writing it down found three things the code did not do:

  · A circle dragged into place sprang back to the packed layout on the
    next live refresh — every twenty seconds while music plays. A moved
    circle now keeps where it was put.
  · There was one "Reset", and it threw away months of listening. Undoing
    an arrangement and destroying the measurements should never be the
    same button: "Reset view" clears pins and positions and appears only
    when there is something to clear.
  · Playing a circle was shift-click or the P key, and the hint mentioned
    neither. Double-click plays, and the hint says what all three
    gestures do.

Also: the connection readout is now a polite live region, so a reconnect
that failed is announced rather than only drawn.

README covers the four features, and its "no network requests" claim is
corrected — that is now true of the shipping configuration rather than of
the app unconditionally, and the difference is worth stating precisely.

